ze people to  move to electricity by making them pay  more for using gas but will this work  and is it fair who should pay for  greening our homes david to you first  i think we know a few things the first  is that this transition needs to happen  a recent study i saw suggested that  actually twice as much emissions are  coming from home heaters as they're  coming from all of the country's power  missions we know that the country will  be better off and prices will be cheaper  in the medium and long term if we make  this transition and we know to this  point that the prices that we have been  paying don't reflect the true cost of  burning these fuels i think globally the  imf believes that fossil fuels are being  subsidized to the tune of five or six  trillion  dollars every year um to me the answer  is quite clear that the government's  role is to make sure that things that  look appealing and attractive in the  medium and long term can be afforded  today and i think for that reason um  it's the government's role to be sure  now um taxes can play a role there but  i'd like to see the uk government also  doing more subsidy on the uptake and on  the electricity end so that it's not  just a stick approach but also a carrot  approach  so who should be paying for it  governments  consumers or industry alice 28 of  homeowners in the uk don't plan to  eco-upgrade their homes at tax  incentives the answer  i mean look i can i can speak from the  perspective of someone through my work  and in photography and filmmaking who's  been traveling to climate front lines  and i can completely understand for uk  homeowners this issue might feel distant  abstract coming far down the line but we  really  have an incredible amount of evidence  we've had it for decades and if we  needed any more there have been historic  flooding in germany historic wildfires  across california  i i would ask what other choice we have  than to lead this transition and and i  would speak of our sort of massive  responsibility to do so and to do so as  quickly as possible um you know the rate  of our we are in the uk the home of the  industrial revolution we began this sort  of hugely  uh  misguided fossil fuel experiment um and  now i really believe that it's our role  to lead the transition out of it is it  going to be easy to retrofit 29 million  homes  absolutely not um but i would say that  the cost of inaction far outweighs  uh the cost of action and and you know  as as david says and and as he painted  so powerfully in his book this is  literally talking about trillions of  damage if we don't take action  and just briefly david before we move on  how does the u.s compare by comparison  america huge energy consumers but also  huge potential for renewables  well we're not doing nearly as well as  the uk uh we have a much smaller share  of renewable power um but we're on the  brink i hope of making massive  investments here to green our grid um  with the with the infrastructure  reconciliation bills that are being  talked about right now so we're i would  say we're lagging behind um but i i hope  that if we were talking a month from now  the picture would look a lot greener  okay just moving on to our next big  subject of the day a week after prime  minister boris johnson said humanity is  reaching a turning point on climate  change there's been a warning that  people's anxiety levels over the issue  is reaching an all-time high could  scaring people too much  end up preventing them from taking  action  how do we find that balance how should  we deal with climate anxiety alice  well the way we talk about climate is  something i'm i'm very preoccupied with  how do we tell this story and how do we  make people feel um  powerful and and not powerless i'm not  sure i have the answer but should we be  concerned about eco anxiety 100  um and i would say that  feeling anxiety in the face of headlines  like uh you know the ipcc's red code for  humanity scientists telling us untold  suffering is coming down the line if we  don't take action these are entirely  rational responses to the information um  that we are learning i'm hearing about  the rise of climate cafes across the uk  sort of workshop therapy sessions for  for young people and and i i can't help  but think about you know not so long ago  young people when they thought about the  future um it was a future of flying cars  and and now it's  one that is very dystopian but we have  to talk not only about the sacrifice and  not only about the dystopia but also  about the opportunities that we have to  build a better and fairer and greener  world  as you mentioned there alice young  people certainly are the demographic  that are going to have to deal with  climate change for longer and the  demographic that are more concerned  about it 56  of young people according to a recent  poll think humanity is doomed david how  would you reassure them  i think the opportunities are there to  make sure the future is much more  comfortable and much more attractive  than the one they imagine reading the  climate science headlines that's because  dramatic change is quite possible it's  not because the climate science is  um  you know being presented in a too scary  manner that is what the science says we  don't change course we don't take action  the world will not resemble the one that  our parents and grandparents and  great-grandparents lived in um  the  what we would have to do to live  comfortably in that world is far beyond  our imaginations and we have to take  seriously just how urgent the crisis is  if we hope to respond to it for those  who worry about climate anxiety i think  it is real for people who are  preoccupied with the subject but i also  think it is a motivator i think if we  look at the last few years um after the  ipc's 1.5 degree report we saw the rise  of brother tinberg and the um the school  strikers we saw the rise of extinction  rebellion in the u.s we saw the rise of  sunrise and all of global climate  geopolitics has changed in the aftermath  of those protest movements which were  motivated by fear they also offered  positive visions of the future i think  those are important too but they were  driven primarily by anxiety i think we  should respond to how scary the science  is by mobilizing not by um collapsing  into despair but i think we have to take  seriously just how urgent the science  says the matter really is  and alice where would you strike the  balance just briefly before we let you  go  the chair of the climate change  committee said if you scare the if you  scare the public you don't get people to  act do you believe that's true  uh i think that  as david says it's it's a motivating  factor for me action is the antidote to  anxiety so you know many of us may act  from fear um it is  not so productive to act from a place of  fear so how can you transform that  emotion into something productive  be an activist whatever that means to  you it doesn't necessarily mean standing  out on the street in protest um it means  different things to all of us but find  what you're good at find what you can  contribute to the movement and play your  part alice aidy and david wallis wells  thank you both very much indeed for  joining us today
 

